WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.158
A consistent watch. Can it measure a track?

00:00:03.158 --> 00:00:06.316
A stable answer may address the wrong question.

00:00:06.316 --> 00:00:09.474
The 2014 Testing Standards distinguish reliability and validity.

00:00:09.474 --> 00:00:12.632
Consistency and measurement error need examination.

00:00:12.632 --> 00:00:15.789
Score interpretations and uses need supporting evidence.

00:00:15.789 --> 00:00:22.105
Varying readings of one stick suggest measurement error.

00:00:22.105 --> 00:00:28.421
With stable readings, examine the intended interpretation.

00:00:28.421 --> 00:00:34.737
Different running speeds prevent time from directly giving distance.

00:00:34.737 --> 00:00:41.053
Ruler and watch are teaching analogies.

00:00:41.053 --> 00:00:44.211
A repeated label does not guarantee validity.

00:00:44.211 --> 00:00:47.368
New ages, languages or uses need relevant evidence.

00:00:47.368 --> 00:00:50.526
Professional assessment combines several information sources.

00:00:50.526 --> 00:00:56.842
Ask what the test claims to measure.

00:00:56.842 --> 00:01:03.158
Look for population, scoring, error and intended use.

00:01:03.158 --> 00:01:06.316
This lesson does not administer a psychological scale.

00:01:06.316 --> 00:01:09.474
A person deserves fuller understanding than one score.

00:01:09.474 --> 00:01:12.632
Questions about measurement are part of scientific literacy.

00:01:12.632 --> 00:01:15.789
Give each score its evidence and limits.
